                               RIPSLIDE v1.0
                                 *Freeware*
                      Copyright ©1994, Michael Nelson


        This may just be the first RIP only Amiga bbs CLI door... While the
object of the game is simple and familiar, it still is a challenge. It is
also kind of fun seeing a RIP bbs door on Amiga bbs's for the first time!


Distribution:

        You can distribute this program to who ever and where ever you
like. The door is freeware so you are not expected to do anything, I
would appreciate any suggestions or comments you may have though!


Installation:

        Set your bbs software to run ripslide as a CLI door. No command
line parameters are required. The door understands the CTRL-C method of
quiting the program (usually CTRL-C is sent by the BBS software only).

        The door is only meant for RIPTERM's (like the one I wrote called
RIPCOMM) and it makes use of some commands which earlier versions of my
RIPCOMM program did not support... so if a user tells you it doesn't
work for him/her, make sure they are using at least version 0.15 of the
RIPCOMM program... if they are NOT using RIPCOMM please tell me about
their problem.

        If ANSI callers try accessing this door, they will see a message
saying a RIPTERM is required in order to play.


Note to Excelsior! sysops:

        I tried the door using a demo of E!, and I guess the local console
doesn't support RIP autodetecting? Since the door didn't get a reply. This
was the 1.20 demo version that I tried). Since E! is the only bbs program to
support RIP locally, I'm willing to fix it... all I need is a command line
option (or whatever) to tell when the local console is in use or that
the sysop is using the door, so I can bypass the autodetect! Email me the
info if your interested.


Programming Problems:

        I was testing the program using nullmodem.device with the xenolink
bbs software, the door seemed very quick and reponsive... until I tried
installing it on my bbs for real, then everything grinded to halt?
The problem was that I had the local bbs screen open, displaying what I was
doing from my other Amiga using RIPcomm. The bbs would spit out the text to
the local console first and then to the RIPcomm... this takes a few moments.
Shutting down the local console returned things to normal speed! That's
no good, then you can't snoop without people knowing it! <G> I was able to
fix it by doing the following;

             I Write() in small 80 byte chunks, instead of dumping a whole
     RIPscrip file to _stdout... that wasn't the real problem but it
     helped.

             The biggest problem I could see was that while the bbs software
     was dumping the text to the local console, the large text and scrolling
     which occured introduced lengthy lags in the user input/response cycle.

             I then wrote a routine which would parse the text before it
     goes to _stdout converting all linefeed characters ($0A) to Carriage
     returns ($0D) to prevent the bbs console from doing any text scrolling.
     The output on the bbs console will all appear on the first row of the
     screen.


Now the program has virtually NO noticeable slowdowns if the sysops watches
or not. Just a little info you may want to pass on to a door programmer
whose door does what I describe.


Feedback:

        This door has only been tested on Xenolink, but should work on any
bbs program which can use CLI/DOS doors. Let me know if you have any problems
on other bbs software.

        If you install this door on your bbs, I would appreciate it if you
sent me netmail or email... that way I can get a feel of how many sysop
are using it, and whether it's worth the effort to write any more "little"
doors like this.

        Also, tell me what your users think of it... for most of them I'm
sure that the only reason they use the door in the first place is because
it supports RIP.

        If you have any ideas for another game/puzzle which would be easy to
write... send it to me, I'm always looking for something new to work on
(ripslide was the result of about 4 hours work).


Enhancements:

        I was thinking of adding a "competition" mode, where users would
try to beat other users solving times (using the same tile arrangements)?

        The tile scrambling routine is a little lame, it just randomly picks
a tile and then checks if it is moveable.


